hi there today I want to talk about a  concept called inter leaving or  interleaving interleaved practice a  secret' enhanced learning technique to  learn more about it all you have to do  is google interleaving what it means is  and what the this interleaving research  shows is that if we compare two forms of  learning one where you what they call  massively or as a block deliberately try  to learn certain information or a  particular skill sort of massively  deliberately in a focused way and if on  the other hand you have another group of  people who learn one skill or one bit of  information then move on to another bit  of information and so they're sort of  inter layering or eating interleaving  they're learning what happens is  according to the results of a number of  tests that those who study in block  massively will learn that particular bit  of knowledge or skill better in the  short term while they are doing it  they'll learn better than the other  group that are learning in a staggered  or interspersed way but in the long run  the interleaved or staggered learners  will do better and this has and this is  apparently new and they're looking at  the reasons why this tends to open up  connections between neurons in the brain  or whatever but the results without  getting into the neuroscience the  results seem to be quite clear and I you  know googled interleaving and there's a  lot of sites and I read a few of them  and I invite you to go and read it and  to me this justifies and confirms to me  the the validity of what we do at link  because if I look at my own sort of  learning strategies first of all even  before we had link I would typically  by two or three starter books whether it  be SML teach yourself colloquial  not just one but a variety so that I'm  not trying to ace teach yourself and  learn everything and teach yourself all  I'm trying to do is expose myself to  some of the same material in different  sources the second thing is when I study  at link as I've said many times my  initial strategy and my initial goal is  to increase my familiarity with the new  language to acquire a lot of vocabulary  to be able to understand what I read and  listen it's not immediately to be able  to speak again because my experience has  shown that if I understand what I hear  if I have vocabulary if I have developed  familiarity with the language I will be  able to speak and so I can actually  delay starting to speak until later I do  look at grammar rules but again not  massively not with the intent of trying  to learn it and I have tried that I have  tried looking at German declension  tables or Russian declension tables or  French conjugation tables and sit there  and try to get it in my head but it  doesn't work whereas if I casually look  at it as a kind of refresher and then I  go back to reading and listening and I  start to notice some of those situations  over time when I go back for the third  and fourth and fifth time to look at  those rules eventually they start to  relate to something that I actually  experienced while listening and reading  so I am interleaving in that way another  way in which I interleave is that when  I'm reading a text you know first of all  a very easy material and difficult  material when I'm reading difficult  material there are a lot of unknown  words blue words at link so I then have  to deliberately go through and look  those up on the dictionary in the  dictionary which is connected to to link  of course and as we know most things you  look up in the dictionary you will  immediately forget however I've looked  it up in the context of this article  that I'm reading when I look it up the  blue word is converted to yellow when I  next encounter that word in another text  or when I read the same text over again  with all the blue words converted to  yellow I am forced to try to remember  that word which mostly I don't remember  so I click on it and I see it every time  I'm clicking on 