 as exposure as  part of another layer of learning that's  kind of going on in the case of  Ukrainian I haven't yet looked at any  grammar resources because I'm sure that  it differs some from Russian but you  know let me first wait until I have  enough experience with the language then  I'll go and look at how you know  different cases or whatever differences  there are Ukraine now I'll deal with  that later so again it gets back to this  idea of not massively deliberately in a  focused rate trying to learn and master  one thing but rather exposing yourself  to a variety of different things I may  talk to people again I haven't yet  spoken to anyone in Ukraine and I don't  think I will for quite a while but in  the case of Korean in my 90 day  challenge in 90 days I think I had a  total of 13 hours of conversation not an  awful lot but I improved so that was  kind of layered in there as well never  for example with my conversations I  wasn't expecting to do particularly well  or expecting to achieve anything of  course we like to improve but it was  just another form of exposure another  activity I wrote because that was one of  the goals in the 90 day challenge and I  did a bit of that not necessarily  expecting to get it right or anything  but it was all part of this gradual  entered layering inter leaving of  different exposures to the language  always challenging myself because I have  to try to write I have to try to  understand and so forth so I think that  a lot of what we do at Lincoln why it is  so effective I think can be  explaining by these articles on  interleaving now I might be quite  selective in this trying to you know  find a justification for link II where  perhaps there isn't any so I look  forward to counter arguments from people  out there but in any case go have a look  at this whole issue of interleaving I  think it's very interesting interleave  as leaves and leaves on the tree  interleaving so go and have a look and I  look forward to your